<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">9 March in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">9 events that happened on 9 March, from 141 BCE to 1945.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">141 BCE <span class="tih-ago">2,166 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Liu Che ascends the Han throne as Emperor Wu</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Liu Che became the seventh emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, known after his death as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>. His reign of 54 years saw long campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, Han expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the promotion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> in government.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1074">1074 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">952 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Gregory VII's synod condemns married and simoniac priests</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At his Lenten synod in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a> barred married priests and those who had bought office from ministering. The reforms helped impose clerical celibacy in the Western Church.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1230">1230 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">796 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Ivan Asen II routs Theodore of Epirus at Klokotnitsa</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> routed and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-komnenos-doukas">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, who had broken a treaty and invaded Bulgaria. The victory ended Epirote power and brought much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, Macedonia and Albania under Bulgarian rule.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-klokotnitsa">Battle of Klokotnitsa</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1500">1500 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">526 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pedro Álvares Cabral sails from Lisbon</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Cabral's fleet of 13 ships left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, swinging far west into the Atlantic. On 22 April it sighted the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, which Cabral claimed for Portugal.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-alvares-cabral">Pedro Álvares Cabral</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1796">1796 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">230 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Napoleon Bonaparte marries Joséphine de Beauharnais</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The civil wedding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> took place two days before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> left to command the Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Joséphine became empress in 1804, but he divorced her in 1810 for lack of an heir.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1841">1841 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">185 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Supreme Court frees the Amistad Africans</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Court ruled that the Mende captives who had seized the slave ship Amistad had been illegally enslaved. Argued by John Quincy Adams, the case became a cause for abolitionists.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1862">1862 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">164 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Monitor and Virginia fight the first battle between ironclads</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At Hampton Roads, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, USS Monitor and the Confederate CSS Virginia fought for hours without decisive damage. The duel showed that wooden warships were obsolete.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1916">1916 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">110 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pancho Villa raids Columbus, New Mexico</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Some 500 of Villa's fighters attacked the border town, killing 18 Americans. The US sent General John Pershing on a punitive expedition into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, which failed to catch Villa.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1945">1945 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">81 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">US firebombing raid devastates Tokyo</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In the night of 9-10 March, some 300 B-29 bombers dropped incendiaries on Tokyo, burning about 41 square kilometres. Around 100,000 people died, the deadliest air raid in history.</p></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 9 March <span class="n">5</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/aloysius-gonzaga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aloysius-gonzaga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Aloysius Gonzaga</span><span class="tp">1568–1591</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-kleber"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jean-baptiste-kleber.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Jean-Baptiste Kléber</span><span class="tp">1753–1800</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-p-laurel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jose-p-laurel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">José P. Laurel</span><span class="tp">1891–1959</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/matyas-rakosi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/matyas-rakosi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Mátyás Rákosi</span><span class="tp">1892–1971</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/yuri-gagarin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yuri-gagarin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Yuri Gagarin</span><span class="tp">1934–1968</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 9 March <span class="n">3</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/sverre-of-norway"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sverre-of-norway.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Sverre of Norway</span><span class="tp">c. 1150–1202</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/leonardo-bruni.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Leonardo Bruni</span><span class="tp">c. 1370–1444</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-lettow-vorbeck"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/paul-von-lettow-vorbeck.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck</span><span class="tp">1870–1964</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
